  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough continues to gradually shift northeast by \nSaturday evening and into Sunday. As it does so, disturbances \nembedded within southwest flow aloft and increased moisture yield \nincreasing rain chances. The best chances (50-80%) will begin across \nour far western zones Saturday night and become areawide by Sunday \nmorning. At this time, the best chances on Sunday look to be across \nthe Permian Basin and southeast New Mexico. Multiple rounds of heavy \nrainfall is possible for some areas and may result in localized \nflash flooding during this timeframe (especially across portions of \nsoutheast New Mexico where the best lift is expected to be). \n\nOn Monday and Tuesday, the aforementioned upper trough lifts into \nthe central and northern Great Plains, sending a cold front \nsouthward. Additional disturbances within the flow aloft keep rain \nchances in the forecast across much of the area, with the highest \nchances across the higher terrain thanks to southeasterly upslope \nflow/orographic forcing. Chances of rain decrease from west to east \nMonday night into Tuesday as troughing weakens. Subtle ridging \nreturns by midweek, allowing for tempertures to warm up slightly. \nHowever, ridging may stay weak enough to keep low (10-20%) rain \nchances around.\n  \nGreening",
